     You're stuck in single-player land.  When you successfully
use the recover utility, it will indeed make a save file which
reflects the state of play as it was when you entered (or last
re-entered) the current dungeon level.  But he didn't--and
couldn't--use the recover utility because nethack had created
a panic save file and removed the individual level files in the
process.  The save file in that case will reflect the state of
play as it was when the program panicked.  Effectively it is
the same as using the 'S' command except that it occurs in the
middle of the current turn and data written into the file might
already be corrupted depending upon what caused NetHack to
decide that something was drastically wrong.

     Traditionally single user systems have not been allowed
to make panic save files since they were unable to adequately
protect themselves against rogue programs (no pun intended...)
running amuck.  No one wants NetHack to be able to corrupt the
file system because some stray pointer bug screws up the I/O
system and clobbers something that doesn't belong to the current
program.


